@page "/components/ai/generativeuiview"
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject BitAiGenerativeUiRegistry GenerativeRegistry

<PageOutlet Url="components/ai/generativeuiview"
            Title="AiGenerativeUiView"
            Description="BitAiGenerativeUiView renders a component a model chose, bound to the properties it produced - only components the app registered, and only the parameters they declare." />

<DemoPage Name="AiGenerativeUiView"
          Description="BitAiGenerativeUiView renders a component the model chose, with the properties it produced. The model names a component and the view looks the name up in the generative UI registry - never a type name in the payload - so a model can only ever instantiate a component the app deliberately registered, and only set the parameters listed on it. Properties that are still streaming bind as far as they have arrived, so a card fills in as the model writes it; a name nothing is registered under renders a quiet placeholder, and the component runs inside an error boundary, so neither takes the answer around it down."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubAIUrl="GenerativeUiView/BitAiGenerativeUiView.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="AI/GenerativeUiView/BitAiGenerativeUiViewDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.AI" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            BitAiGenerativeUiView resolves components through the <b>IBitAiGenerativeUiRegistry</b> that
            <b>AddBitBlazorUIAIServices</b> registers; the app fills it at startup with <b>ConfigureGenerativeUi</b> (see
            <BitLink Href="#example4">The registry</BitLink>). This site registers one component, <b>forecast_card</b>, which every
            live demo below renders. Inside a <BitLink Href="/components/ai/chat">BitAiChat</BitLink> the view is rendered for
            every generative UI part on its own - see the <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/generative">Generative UI</BitLink> scenario.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            <b>Security</b>: the registration is the boundary. Register only components that are safe to show with any values of
            their parameters, since a model - or whoever can steer its input - picks both. Callbacks, render fragments, the
            attributes catch-all and any type reaching a delegate or a <b>Type</b> are never bindable, whatever is registered.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            <b>Trimming</b>: registering reads the parameter types by reflection, so <b>Register</b> is marked
            <b>[RequiresUnreferencedCode]</b> and <b>[RequiresDynamicCode]</b>. The component type itself is kept by the
            annotation of <b>Register</b>; an app that trims keeps the types its parameters reach (a plain string or number needs
            nothing), and says so with an <b>[UnconditionalSuppressMessage]</b> on the method that registers.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                Give it a <b>Part</b>, a <b>BitAiGenerativeUiPart</b> naming a registered component and carrying its
                properties as JSON. The view finds <b>forecast_card</b> in the registry and renders the app's ForecastCard
                component with the parameters bound from the JSON - here as a session would leave it, finished.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiGenerativeUiView Part="osloPart" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Streaming properties" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                The properties of a part can stream in as raw JSON (<b>AppendProps</b>, what a
                <b>BitAiGenerativeUiDeltaEvent</b> does). The view re-binds them on every change, as far as they have arrived
                - a string even while it is being written - so the card fills in field by field rather than appearing at the end.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton OnClick="StreamForecastAsync" Disabled="isStreamingForecast">Stream a forecast</BitButton>
            <br /><br />
            @if (streamedPart is not null)
            {
                <BitAiGenerativeUiView Part="streamedPart" />
            }
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="From a transport" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example3CodeFiles" Id="example3">
            <div>
                A transport asks for a component with a <b>BitAiGenerativeUiEvent</b> - a name and the properties - and the
                session adds a generative UI part to the answer for each one. The scripted transport here asks for three
                forecast cards between two lines of text.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton OnClick="AskForForecastsAsync" Disabled="forecastSession.IsGenerating">The week ahead in three cities</BitButton>
            <br /><br />
            @if (ForecastAnswer is { } answer)
            {
                @foreach (var part in answer.PartsOf<BitAiGenerativeUiPart>())
                {
                    <BitAiGenerativeUiView Part="part" />
                }
            }
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="The registry" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example4CodeFiles" Id="example4">
            <div>
                <b>BitAiGenerativeUiRegistry.Register&lt;TComponent&gt;(name, description, required)</b>, through
                <b>ConfigureGenerativeUi</b> of <b>AddBitBlazorUIAIServices</b>, lets model output render a component under a
                name. Its bindable properties are its public <b>[Parameter]</b>s, less the ones no model should set: callbacks,
                render fragments, the attributes catch-all, any type whose graph reaches a delegate or a <b>Type</b>, and any
                parameter marked <b>[BitAiGenerativeIgnore]</b> (for one the app sets itself, such as a service key).
                <b>[Description]</b> on a parameter is what the model is told it means; on the component it is the description
                when <b>Register</b> is given none; a name in <b>required</b> - or a <b>[Required]</b> parameter - must be supplied.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <b>DescribeAll</b> writes all of it as one JSON Schema to hand the model (in a system prompt, or as the schema
                of a render tool) - without it the model guesses names, and every guess renders a placeholder. This is the
                schema this site's registry describes:
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiJsonView Value="registrySchema" DefaultExpandDepth="8" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Unregistered components" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                A name nothing is registered under never reaches a component: the view renders a warning tag named after it,
                once the part has finished - while it still streams, the name may be one the model is not done spelling - so
                a component a model made up costs the answer nothing.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiGenerativeUiView Part="unregisteredPart" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Binding diagnostics" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                A value of the wrong shape is skipped rather than thrown, and a property the component does not declare is
                ignored rather than passed - the card renders with what did bind. <b>ShowBindingErrors</b> lists both under the
                component once the part is complete: the errors (a value that did not bind, a required property left out) and
                the ignored names, kept apart since only the first means the card is missing something. Useful while writing
                the prompt, noise in production.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiGenerativeUiView Part="faultyPart" ShowBindingErrors />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                <b>BitParams</b> with a <b>BitAiGenerativeUiViewParams</b> sets the defaults of every view inside it - one place
                to turn <b>ShowBindingErrors</b> on for a whole conversation while a prompt is being developed. A view's own
                value still wins.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitToggle @bind-Value="showDiagnostics" Text="Show binding errors" />
            <BitParams Parameters="generativeParams">
                <BitAiGenerativeUiView Part="faultyPart" />
                <BitAiGenerativeUiView Part="faultyPart" ShowBindingErrors="false" />
            </BitParams>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> reach the root, <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> each part: the container of the
                component, the placeholder tag, the diagnostics and their tags. The component itself is the app's, styled as the
                app styles it. The public <b>--bit-AiGenerativeUiView-*</b> variables (see the CSS variables table) inherit, so
                set on <b>:root</b>, a container or a view's <b>Style</b> they re-skin every view below.
            </div>
            <br />
            @* A plain element of this page for the scoped ::deep rules of the custom classes to anchor on. *@
            <div>
                <BitAiGenerativeUiView Part="osloPart"
                                       Style="padding: 0.5rem; border-radius: 0.5rem; background-color: #8a2be21a"
                                       Styles="@(new() { Content = "display: inline-block; transform: rotate(-2deg)" })" />
                <br />
                <BitAiGenerativeUiView Part="faultyPart" ShowBindingErrors Classes="@(new() { Diagnostics = "custom-diagnostics" })" />
                <br />
                <div style="--bit-AiGenerativeUiView-diagnostics-gap: 0.25rem; --bit-AiGenerativeUiView-diagnostics-margin: 0">
                    <BitAiGenerativeUiView Part="faultyPart" ShowBindingErrors />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
            <div>
                In right-to-left text the container, the placeholder and the diagnostics start on the right, whether the
                direction comes from <b>Dir</b> or from a <b>dir</b> attribute around the view; the component inherits the
                direction like any markup inside it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiGenerativeUiView Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Part="tehranPart" />
            <br />
            <div dir="rtl">
                <BitAiGenerativeUiView Part="unregisteredPart" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
